{%- comment -%}
  SHUDHI — Living Conversation

  The editorial panel that occupies sh-contact's right column: a rotating brand
  statement that responds to what the visitor is doing in the form beside it, a
  slow-breathing status dot, a botanical ring and a field of drifting seeds.

  A SNIPPET, not a section, and not by preference — it renders inside
  .sh-contact__grid as that grid's second child, and a Shopify section cannot
  nest inside another section's grid. All of its settings therefore live in
  sections/sh-contact.liquid's schema (a snippet has none) and arrive here on
  the `s` drop. CSS is shudhi-home.css §24; behaviour is
  assets/sh-living-conversation.js.

  Usage — inside .sh-contact__col--side:
    {%- render 'sh-living-conversation',
          uid: uid, s: s, lc_list: lc_list,
          animate: animate_form, lc_state: lc_state, compact: has_aside -%}

  Params:
    uid      — section.id. `section` is out of scope inside a rendered snippet,
               so the caller has to pass it.
    s        — section.settings.
    lc_list  — the statements, already split by the caller. Split there rather
               than here because the caller needs its .size to decide whether to
               render this snippet at all, and parsing it twice would let the
               two answers drift.
    animate  — sh-contact's `animate_form`. ONE flag, two consumers; see note 3.
    lc_state — 'idle' | 'sent' | 'error', resolved from form.posted_successfully?
               and form.errors by the caller.
    compact  — true when the contact-details card shares this column.

  Five things here are deliberate and easy to undo by accident:

  1. THE WHOLE PANEL IS aria-hidden="true", AND THAT IS THE CORRECT ANSWER, not
     a concession. snippets/sh-gen-seal.liquid:54-60 already makes this call for
     its decorative number, and sh-contact.liquid note 4 makes the other half of
     it ("one reliable announcement beats two unreliable ones"). A live region
     re-announcing a statement every seven seconds — and again on every field
     focus — would interrupt the announcement of the label of the field the user
     just tabbed into. That is a misuse of SC 4.1.3, not a satisfaction of it.
     The panel carries no information the form does not already carry: the
     statements are atmosphere, and the status line duplicates what the
     autofocused <h2> and .sh-form__ok already say on the success render.

     It also disposes of the usual character-splitting problem. Screen readers
     read one clean sentence — the form's — whether or not JS has split this
     one, so there is nothing to restore with an sr-only copy.

  2. pointer-events:none in §24 is load-bearing, not cosmetic. axe's
     aria-hidden-focus rule flags TABBABLE content inside an aria-hidden
     subtree; that rule plus "no focusable descendant here" is what keeps this
     passing, the same way the honeypot's tabindex="-1" does at :2790.
     *** If a link, button or input is ever added inside this panel, BOTH
     aria-hidden and pointer-events:none must go, and the accessibility model
     has to be redesigned before it does. ***

  3. `animate` is sh-contact's own animate_form, not a separate setting. On the
     success and error renders that flag is false, so this panel ships without
     data-reveal, renders at full opacity with no entrance, and the JS reads
     hasAttribute('data-reveal') and skips the entrance sequence entirely. The
     confirmation heading is the autofocus target and must be the only thing
     moving — a decorative panel choreographing itself next to it is exactly the
     distraction note 3 in sh-contact.liquid forbids.

  4. The first statement is printed as PLAIN TEXT, and the status line is
     printed SERVER-SIDE from lc_state. Both are readable with JS off, blocked,
     or still deferred. JS splits the line into per-character spans and adds
     .is-split to the root; every char-level rule in §24 is scoped under that
     class, so an unsplit line has no motion styles to flash out of. The base
     state of a split char is VISIBLE — the animations are `backwards`/`forwards`
     filled overlays on top of it, never a pre-state that has to be removed.

  5. Seed positions come from a hand-authored table, not Math.random(). Liquid
     has no rand, and that is a feature: composed dust reads as design where
     random dust reads as noise, and a server-rendered position is stable across
     a reload or a back-navigation, where a re-randomised field reads as a
     glitch. The durations are mutually prime-ish so the field never syncs into
     a visible collective pulse.

  Copy is stored in SENTENCE case and set in caps by §24's text-transform.
  Storing caps would bake a presentation decision into the merchant's data —
  and if this panel ever stopped being aria-hidden, screen readers spell
  all-caps words out letter by letter.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign lc_uid   = uid      | default: 1 -%}
{%- assign lc_st    = lc_state | default: 'idle' -%}

{%- comment -%} The opening line answers the render it is on, so a visitor who
    just submitted is not greeted by an ambient statement about starting a
    conversation. Falls back to the first rotating statement. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign lc_first = lc_list[0] | strip -%}
{%- if lc_st == 'sent' and s.lc_statement_sent != blank -%}
  {%- assign lc_first = s.lc_statement_sent -%}
{%- elsif lc_st == 'error' and s.lc_statement_error != blank -%}
  {%- assign lc_first = s.lc_statement_error -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- case lc_st -%}
  {%- when 'sent'  -%}{%- assign lc_status = s.lc_status_sent  -%}
  {%- when 'error' -%}{%- assign lc_status = s.lc_status_error -%}
  {%- else         -%}{%- assign lc_status = s.lc_status_idle  -%}
{%- endcase -%}

{%- comment -%} Milliseconds for the JS; the setting is in seconds because that
    is what a merchant thinks in. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign lc_ms = s.lc_interval | default: 7 | times: 1000 -%}

{%- comment -%} x% | y% | scale | drift seconds | delay seconds | opacity.
    Fourteen beside the panel alone, the first six when the details card shares
    the column. §24 drops the layer entirely below 768px. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign seed_rows = '29,71,1,17,2.4,.30|12,22,.7,13,0,.22|68,31,.85,19,3.6,.26|82,64,.6,14,.7,.20|21,48,.75,23,4.9,.24|57,86,.9,16,2.1,.28|44,14,.55,11,1.1,.18|89,18,.5,12,5.5,.16|7,63,.8,21,1.6,.22|74,79,.65,15,3.1,.19|38,35,.6,18,6.2,.17|63,55,.7,13,4.2,.21|16,88,.85,20,.4,.25|92,42,.55,22,5.1,.18' | split: '|' -%}
{%- if compact -%}
  {%- assign seed_count = 6 -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign seed_count = 14 -%}
{%- endif -%}

<div class="sh-lc"
     id="sh-lc-{{ lc_uid }}"
     aria-hidden="true"
     data-lc-state="{{ lc_st }}"
     data-lc-interval="{{ lc_ms }}"
     data-lc-name="{{ s.lc_on_name | escape }}"
     data-lc-email="{{ s.lc_on_email | escape }}"
     data-lc-phone="{{ s.lc_on_phone | escape }}"
     data-lc-order="{{ s.lc_on_order | escape }}"
     data-lc-reason="{{ s.lc_on_reason | escape }}"
     data-lc-message="{{ s.lc_on_message | escape }}"
     data-lc-typing="{{ s.lc_on_typing | escape }}"
     data-lc-typing2="{{ s.lc_on_typing_2 | escape }}"
     data-lc-typing3="{{ s.lc_on_typing_3 | escape }}"
     data-lc-status-idle="{{ s.lc_status_idle | escape }}"
     data-lc-status-typing="{{ s.lc_status_typing | escape }}"
     data-lc-status-sending="{{ s.lc_status_sending | escape }}"
     {% if animate %}data-reveal{% endif %}>

  {%- comment -%} Decorative layers, behind the copy and clipped to the panel so
      a drifting seed can never widen the grid column. {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="sh-lc__art">

    {%- comment -%} Two thin curves hugging the LEFT and RIGHT margins of the
        panel, deliberately not behind the type: a stroke crossing an uppercase
        serif line reads as a printing fault, not as decoration. They frame the
        column instead.

        pathLength="1" normalises each path to a single user unit, so §24's
        stroke-dasharray:1 / dashoffset:1->0 draws it exactly, with no
        getTotalLength() and therefore no JavaScript at all.

        UNIFORM scaling — the default preserveAspectRatio, and no
        vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke". Both of those were tried and both are
        wrong here, for one reason each. non-scaling-stroke moves the stroke into
        SCREEN space, which takes the dash pattern with it while pathLength keeps
        normalising in USER space: the two disagree and every arc renders as a
        run of 1px dashes instead of a line. preserveAspectRatio="none" then
        needed that vector-effect to stop the non-uniform scale squashing the
        stroke into a wedge. The viewBox is simply given the panel's own
        proportions instead, so the default "meet" lands within a pixel or two of
        edge-to-edge and every one of those problems disappears. {%- endcomment -%}
    <svg class="sh-lc__arcs" viewBox="0 0 82 100" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path class="sh-lc__arc" pathLength="1" d="M 2,93 C 4,74 6,56 9,37"/>
      <path class="sh-lc__arc" pathLength="1" d="M 80,35 C 78,54 76,72 73,92"/>
      <path class="sh-lc__arc" pathLength="1" d="M 9,37 l 4,2 M 9,37 l -1,4.5"/>
    </svg>

    {%- if s.lc_dust -%}
      <div class="sh-lc__dust sh-lc__depth" data-lc-depth="5.1">
        {%- for row in seed_rows limit: seed_count -%}
          {%- assign p = row | split: ',' -%}
          <i class="sh-lc__seed" style="--x:{{ p[0] }}%;--y:{{ p[1] }}%;--s:{{ p[2] }};--dur:{{ p[3] }}s;--del:{{ p[4] }}s;--o:{{ p[5] }};"></i>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

  </div>

  <div class="sh-lc__copy sh-lc__depth" data-lc-depth="1.5">
    {%- if s.lc_eyebrow != blank -%}
      <p class="sh-lc__eyebrow">{{ s.lc_eyebrow }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} Plain text, deliberately. See note 4. {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="sh-lc__statement" data-lc-stage>
      <span class="sh-lc__line" data-lc-line>{{ lc_first }}</span>
    </p>

    {%- if s.lc_support != blank -%}
      <p class="sh-lc__support">{{ s.lc_support }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <p class="sh-lc__status">
      <i class="sh-lc__dot"></i><span data-lc-status>{{ lc_status }}</span>
    </p>

    {%- if s.lc_symbol -%}
      {%- comment -%} In the flow, under the status line, rather than behind the
          type. Sat behind it the ring crossed every line of an uppercase serif
          statement and read as a printing fault; here it closes the composition
          the way a seal closes a letter.

          Two nested elements on purpose. The OUTER one is what the entrance
          animates (§24 gives every .sh-lc__copy child an opacity/translateY),
          and the INNER one is what the cursor loop writes an inline transform
          to. One element doing both would let the inline transform overwrite
          the entrance mid-reveal — an element cannot hold two transforms.
          {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="sh-lc__seal">
        <div class="sh-lc__symbol sh-lc__depth" data-lc-depth="2.6">
          {%- comment -%} Two <svg> ELEMENTS, counter-rotating. The rotation is
              on the element and never on a <g> or <circle> inside it: on the
              element the default transform-origin:50% 50% resolves against its
              border box with no transform-box declaration needed, which is the
              construction sh-gen__ring already relies on. A square viewBox keeps
              that centre honest at every clamp width. {%- endcomment -%}
          <svg class="sh-lc__ring" viewBox="0 0 240 240" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <circle cx="120" cy="120" r="104"/>
            <circle class="sh-lc__seed-dot" cx="224" cy="120" r="3"/>
            <circle class="sh-lc__seed-dot" cx="120" cy="224" r="3"/>
            <circle class="sh-lc__seed-dot" cx="16"  cy="120" r="3"/>
            <circle class="sh-lc__seed-dot" cx="120" cy="16"  r="3"/>
          </svg>
          <svg class="sh-lc__ring2" viewBox="0 0 240 240" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <circle cx="120" cy="120" r="82"/>
          </svg>

          {%- comment -%} A SIBLING of both rings, never a child: anything inside
              one rides the rotation and spends half the cycle upside down. The
              same call sh-gen-seal.liquid:63-67 makes for its number. {%- endcomment -%}
          {% render 'sh-icon', name: 'sprout', class: 'sh-lc__glyph', sw: 1.1 %}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} The rotation source, read by the JS via textContent. A hidden
      element rather than a JSON <script> block: Liquid's `json` filter does not
      escape "</script>", so a merchant statement containing that sequence would
      close the block and dump the rest of the list into the page as markup. One
      span per statement needs no parsing at all and cannot be broken by any
      character a merchant can type. {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="sh-lc__source" hidden>
    {%- for st in lc_list -%}
      {%- assign st = st | strip -%}
      {%- if st != blank -%}<span data-lc-src>{{ st }}</span>{%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
</div>
